# Grandma Ruth 103 · ANGLE: family secret (daughter-in-law throws away the old recipe box; what was hidden inside)

sc("""Sweetheart, two weeks before Christmas, my daughter-in-law cleaned out my kitchen while I was in the hospital with pneumonia, and she threw my mother's old tin recipe box in the trash. She said it was clutter. Greasy old cards nobody could read. What she didn't know was what my mother had hidden behind those recipe cards in nineteen sixty-one. And neither did I.""",
   "old tin recipe box in trash can", "SHE THREW IT AWAY")
sc("""Tonight I'm going to tell you how I got that box back, what my granddaughter found taped inside the lid on Christmas Eve, and the letter in my mother's handwriting that made my whole family cry around the kitchen table. Stay with me, honey, because the last recipe card in that box wasn't a recipe at all.""",
   "old handwritten recipe cards spread on table", "NOT A RECIPE AT ALL")

ch("The box")
sc("""My mother's recipe box was a red tin box with a little painted Dutch girl on the lid, honey, worn down to bare metal at the corners from sixty years of use. Inside were about two hundred index cards in her handwriting, and her mother's, and some in mine. Spattered with butter and vanilla and grease. Pot roast. Fudge. Molasses cookies. Banana bread. Divinity. My mother's Christmas rolls. Every recipe I've ever shown you came out of that box.""",
   "red tin recipe box with painted Dutch girl", "TWO HUNDRED CARDS")
sc("""When my mother died in nineteen eighty-nine, that box was the only thing I asked for. My brother Danny took the furniture. I took the box. It sat on my kitchen counter by the stove for thirty-five years, and I looked something up in it almost every day. I never cleaned it out. I never went all the way to the back. I didn't need to. In a minute I'll tell you what was in the back.""",
   "recipe box on kitchen counter by stove", "NEVER TO THE BACK")

sc("""My mother learned to cook from her mother, Grandma Pearl, on a farm with no electricity, honey. Grandma Pearl couldn't read very well, so she never wrote anything down. My mother would stand next to her at the wood stove and write it all down on scraps of paper. A handful of this. A pinch of that. Butter the size of an egg. Then she'd copy it onto cards at night. That's how the box started.""",
   "young woman writing recipe beside old woman at wood stove", "BUTTER THE SIZE OF AN EGG")
sc("""Some of those cards had more than recipes on them, sweetheart. On the back of the fudge card, my mother wrote, Ruthie's first time, she burned it, 1951. On the pot roast card, Walter's favorite, never use the cheap onions. On the molasses cookie card, in my handwriting, from when I was twelve, too much clove! Every card was a little diary. Our whole family was in that box.""",
   "back of old recipe card with handwritten note", "A LITTLE DIARY")
ch("The hospital")
sc("""In December of two thousand twenty-three, I got pneumonia, sweetheart. Bad pneumonia, at seventy-nine. I was in the hospital for eleven days. And while I was there, my son's wife, Denise, decided to help. She went to my house with a box of trash bags, and she cleaned out my kitchen. She meant well, honey. I want to say that. But she didn't ask. She wanted me to come home to a clean, modern kitchen, like the ones on television.""",
   "elderly woman in hospital bed with oxygen", "SHE DIDN'T ASK")
sc("""She threw away my old spice tins, my chipped mixing bowls, my mother's rolling pin with the cracked handle, and a drawer of rubber bands and twist ties. And she threw away the recipe box. She said later, Mom, those cards were filthy. You can't even read half of them. I'll type up the good ones for you on the computer. And she put it in a black trash bag on the curb for Thursday pickup.""",
   "black trash bags on curb in winter", "SHE'D TYPE THEM UP")
sc("""If you're enjoying Grandma's story, tap the like button for me, sweetheart. It helps more people find these stories. I came home on a Wednesday afternoon. I walked into my kitchen, and I knew in one second. The empty spot on the counter by the stove. I asked Denise where my mother's box was. And she said, oh, I tossed it. It's in the bags on the curb. Pickup's tomorrow.""",
   "empty spot on kitchen counter", "THE EMPTY SPOT")

ch("The curb")
sc("""I went out to that curb in my bathrobe and slippers, honey, in the snow, seventy-nine years old and two days out of the hospital. And I started tearing open those trash bags with my bare hands. Denise ran out after me yelling, Mom, Mom, you're going to get sick again! And my son came out, and my granddaughter Lily, who was sixteen. And they all stood there watching me.""",
   "elderly woman in bathrobe tearing trash bags in snow", "IN MY BATHROBE")
sc("""It was in the fourth bag, under the coffee grounds. The lid was dented, and some of the cards were wet and stained. I sat right down on the curb in the snow and held it against my chest and cried, sweetheart. Not because of the cards. Because of my mother. That box was the last thing in the world that still had her handwriting in it.""",
   "elderly woman sitting on curb holding recipe box", "HER HANDWRITING")
sc("""Lily sat down next to me on the curb. She put her arm around me. And she said, Grandma, let me help you dry them. Every single one. And Denise stood there on the sidewalk, and her face crumpled. She said, Mom, I'm so sorry. I didn't know. I thought they were just old recipes. And I said, honey, nothing in a kitchen is just old. That's the whole point of a kitchen.""",
   "teenage granddaughter comforting grandmother", "NOTHING IS JUST OLD")

sc("""And here's the thing, honey. I wasn't angry at Denise. Not really. She came from a family where nobody cooked. Her mother worked two jobs and they ate from cans and boxes. She didn't know what a recipe box meant, because nobody had ever given her one. You can't miss what you've never had. That night, I decided I was going to give her one.""",
   "elderly woman thinking by window at night", "SHE NEVER HAD ONE")
ch("Drying the cards")
sc("""So far, the hospital, the curb, and the fourth bag. That night, Lily and I spread every one of those two hundred cards out on my dining room table, on towels, to dry. One by one. And as we laid them out, I told her the story behind each one. The fudge my mother made the Christmas my father lost his job. The pot roast for every Sunday for forty years.""",
   "recipe cards drying on towels on dining table", "THE STORY OF EACH ONE")
sc("""And then, at the very back of the box, behind the last card, Lily found something I'd never seen in thirty-five years. The cardboard lining of the box was loose. And behind it, taped flat against the bottom of the tin, was an envelope. Yellowed and brittle. And on the front, in my mother's careful handwriting, it said, For Ruthie. Open when you need it most.""",
   "yellowed envelope taped inside tin box", "OPEN WHEN YOU NEED IT")

ch("The envelope")
sc("""My hands shook so hard Lily had to open it for me. Inside were three things, honey. A savings bond, from nineteen sixty-one, for two hundred and fifty dollars. That was a fortune for my mother. And a little lock of fine blonde baby hair, tied with a pink thread. Mine. And a letter. Two pages, in her handwriting, dated December the twentieth, nineteen sixty-one.""",
   "old savings bond lock of hair and letter", "THREE THINGS")
sc("""Lily read it out loud to me, sweetheart, at my dining room table, with the cards drying all around us. My mother wrote, Ruthie, you're seventeen, and you think I'm old-fashioned, and you're probably right. I've saved this bond a dollar at a time, from my egg money, for five years. Your father doesn't know. It's for you. Not for a dress or a car. For a day when you're scared and alone and you need your mother.""",
   "teenage girl reading letter aloud to grandmother", "FROM MY EGG MONEY")
sc("""And then she wrote, I'm hiding it in my recipe box, because you'll find it when you finally start cooking for your own family. Every recipe in here is a way of saying I love you. When you make my fudge for your children, you're saying it for me. When I'm gone, I'll still be in your kitchen, every single day. Love, Mama.""",
   "old letter in mother's handwriting close up", "STILL IN YOUR KITCHEN")

sc("""There was a postscript, too, sweetheart. At the bottom of the second page, my mother had written, P.S. If you're reading this, you've finally cleaned out my recipe box, and you're probably standing in your kitchen crying. Go make the fudge. And then call your brother. You two fight too much. Lily laughed so hard she had to put the letter down. Because Danny and I hadn't spoken in three months.""",
   "girl laughing reading old letter", "THEN CALL YOUR BROTHER")
sc("""So that's exactly what I did, honey. At ten o'clock at night, two days out of the hospital, I made my mother's fudge, with Lily stirring. And then I called my brother Danny, in Florida, seventy-six years old. I read him the letter over the phone. And my little brother cried, and I cried, and we made up whatever it was we'd been fighting about. I can't even remember now.""",
   "elderly woman on phone crying happy in kitchen", "I CALLED MY BROTHER")
ch("Sixty-two years")
sc("""She'd hidden it sixty-two years before, honey. And I never found it. Because I never went all the way to the back. I'd been cooking from that box every day for thirty-five years, and she'd been right there under my hand the whole time. Waiting for the day I needed her most. And that day turned out to be two days out of the hospital, at seventy-nine, crying on a curb in the snow.""",
   "elderly woman holding letter crying at table", "WAITING THE WHOLE TIME")
sc("""The savings bond had grown, too, sweetheart. Those old bonds stopped earning interest after forty years, but when my son took it to the bank, it was still worth over a thousand dollars. And I knew exactly what to do with it. I gave it to Lily, who had sat on that curb with me, for college. She's studying to be a nurse. She said, Grandma, it's the egg money. I'll make it count.""",
   "granddaughter hugging grandmother with envelope", "I'LL MAKE IT COUNT")

ch("Denise")
sc("""And Denise, honey? On Christmas Eve, she came to my house with a big flat package. Inside was a frame, and in it, behind glass, was my mother's letter and the lock of hair, preserved so they'd never fall apart. And she said, Mom, I was wrong. I want to learn the recipes. All of them. In your kitchen. In her handwriting. Will you teach me?""",
   "framed old letter and lock of hair as gift", "WILL YOU TEACH ME?")
sc("""Every Saturday since, Denise comes to my kitchen, and we make one recipe from that box. We're up to card ninety-four. She's a good cook, sweetheart, better than me with pie crust. And last month, she taped an envelope inside the lid of her own new recipe box, for her daughter. She won't tell anybody what's in it. That's how it should be.""",
   "two women baking together in kitchen", "CARD NINETY-FOUR")

ch("Grandma's lesson")
sc("""Here's what I want you to remember, honey. The old things in your kitchen aren't clutter. They're letters from people who loved you, written in butter and flour. Before you throw anything away this Christmas, go all the way to the back. Tell me in the comments, sweetheart, do you have your mother's or grandmother's recipes? There are more of my family's stories in the playlist right there on your screen. Good night.""",
   "red tin recipe box on kitchen counter", "GO ALL THE WAY TO THE BACK")

